learnings:
  - A tamper demo that only flips one artifact's bytes proves fail-closed against a LAZY attacker, not a coherent one — the real threat edits the strand AND the thing that pins it (manifest, or an excluded binding). Always exploit the co-tamper.
  - Excluding a field from the identity hash (binding, merge inputs) for legitimate mutability reasons means it needs a SEPARATE authenticated anchor, or it is forgeable — 'excluded from pickId' silently means 'unauthenticated' unless something else pins it.
  - The v1/v2 boundary anchors only the v1 TIP; a prefix of self-hashing strands is content-forgeable, not merely order-forgeable — the header caveat understated the real exposure.
